  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA mid/upper level circulation is located over AR, and this will be \nthe main feature of the pattern across the region into early next \nweek. The low will slowly track east today, putting our area in a \nmidlevel deformation zone. The high PW and weak flow aloft points to \nanother day of scattered to numerous showers and storms that have \nlittle movement, initiating over the higher terrain and mainly \npropagating into the Valley with new development on outflow \nboundaries. As we have seen for the past few days, these slow-moving \nstorms can produce locally heavy rainfall and gusty winds - expect \nthe same potential today. Sunday looks similar as the closed low \ntransitions to an open wave, but still remains over West/Middle TN. \nPW values remain in the 1.8-2 inch range with 1000-1500 J/kg MLCAPE \nand negligible shear. The trough moves over the area on Monday, \nwhich may give a little better instability and shear, particularly \nin the southern half. \n\nBy Tuesday, the pattern will become more northwesterly with the \ntrough to our east and a high pressure ridge to our west. This \nbrings some drier air aloft to the area, along with some steeper mid-\nlevel lapse rates. These storms could pose a little more of an \norganized severe threat, although shear still looks weak. For \nWednesday and beyond, we will remain in a NW flow with embedded \nshortwave troughs bringing some daily chances of showers and storms. \nSpecifics on timing and severe/flooding potential are uncertain at \nthis time.",
